### What version of the Codex App are you using (From “About Codex” dialog)?

26.930.41038, verified from the installed package `openai-codex-desktop 26.930.41038-1` rather than the About dialog. Component: the desktop app's built-in browser.

### What subscription do you have?

Not included. The reproduction uses local webpages and manual clicks, with no model request.

### What platform is your computer?

`uname -mprs`: `Linux 7.2.5-3-omarchy x86_64 unknown`.

Linux, Omarchy 4.0.4-1, Hyprland 0.56.2-2. The desktop app is an XWayland client, verified with `hyprctl -j clients`.

The problem was reproduced on regular and special workspaces. macOS, Windows, and other Linux compositors have not been tested for this reproduction.

### What issue are you seeing?

A user-initiated `window.open()` with popup-size features creates a live child, but returning focus to the desktop app hides that child. Focusing another application on the same workspace makes the child visible again.

The child finishes loading and `child.closed` remains `false`. In the hidden state it reports `document.visibilityState === "hidden"` and `document.hasFocus() === false`.

The child is not exposed as another built-in browser tab. We could not identify it as a distinct managed child in `hyprctl -j clients`, while the ordinary Chromium controls do create distinct managed children. This observation does not establish the internal hosting mechanism.

This is an app/Hyprland interoperability report. The exact defect is unconfirmed, and it should not be attributed to Hyprland alone: ordinary Chromium works under the same compositor in both native Wayland and XWayland controls.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

Save these two files, serve them with `python3 -m http.server 8765`, and open `http://127.0.0.1:8765/` in the unmodified app's built-in browser.

1. Click Open popup.
2. Focus another application's window on the same workspace.
3. Return focus to the desktop app.
4. Observe the child disappearing when its parent is activated. The event log reports its focus and visibility transitions.

`index.html`:

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<button id="open">Open popup</button>
<pre id="log"></pre>
<script>
  let child;
  const log = text => {
    document.querySelector('#log').textContent += text + '\n';
  };
  document.querySelector('#open').onclick = () => {
    child = window.open('/child.html', 'popup-test',
      'popup,width=560,height=480');
    setTimeout(() => log('closed=' + child?.closed), 500);
  };
  window.addEventListener('message', event => {
    if (event.origin === location.origin && event.source === child) {
      log(event.data);
    }
  });
</script>
```

`child.html`:

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Popup test child</title>
<h1>Popup test child</h1>
<button onclick="window.close()">Close child</button>
<script>
  const report = text => window.opener?.postMessage(text, location.origin);
  report('loaded');
  window.addEventListener('focus', () => report('focus'));
  window.addEventListener('blur', () => report('blur'));
  document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
    report('visibility=' + document.visibilityState);
  });
</script>
```

### What is the expected behavior?

An allowed child remains visible, reachable, and operable when the parent receives focus. If this window mode is unsupported, show an actionable reason rather than keeping a live but inaccessible child.

### Additional information

Control results using the same HTML and compositor:

| Host | Result |
| --- | --- |
| App built-in browser, XWayland | Child hides when the parent receives focus |
| Ordinary Chromium, native Wayland | Independent tiled child works after parent refocus |
| Ordinary Chromium, XWayland with an isolated temporary profile | Independent tiled child works after parent refocus; both windows verified as XWayland managed clients |

A further control using `window.open('/child.html', '_blank', 'popup=no,width=560,height=480')` opened an operable built-in tab. Separate same-origin tests on that route confirmed a non-null opener, child-to-parent DOM changes and function calls, postMessage with matching source/origin, and the parent's retained child reference. Those results support a tab-disposition workaround; they do not prove every popup workflow is compatible.

A local isolated-profile proof of concept also let the user choose Window (default) or Tab under Settings > Browser > General. The user confirmed the tab route worked. Please consider this as an optional workaround while fixing popup ownership/visibility: keep the current window behavior by default and retain existing opener, noopener/noreferrer, origin, and navigation policies.

Related Windows report: #46469. The original Linux observations were added at https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/46469#issuecomment-6341136903 . This standalone issue separates the reproducible Linux case from that Windows report. A shared cause has NOT been established.

The separate `window.prompt()` failure reproduces without this popup sequence and is reported in #56186.

No modified application, extracted application source, authentication data, raw logs, or private screenshots are attached. The baseline reproduction and Chromium comparisons were performed before local patch experiments.
